    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d652">

                <pb facs="0735" n="725" sameAs="frus:frus1943China/pg725"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15192: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-12-04T16:00:00+07:00">December 4, 1943—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 5:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2325. In Kunming’s airgram A–6, December 1<anchor n="18" xml:id="d652-f1"/>
                    monthly political review, appears a statement that the trial of the bandits who
                    attacked the convoy is drawing to a close and that the provincial chairman has
                        <pb facs="0736" n="726" xml:id="pg726"/> promised that summary punishment
                    will be meted out to the bandits and the delinquent magistrates (Embassy’s
                    despatch 1765, November 3). This is the first information received by Embassy
                    indicating that any of the bandits had been apprehended or that any forthright
                    action was being taken in the matter. We are asking Kunming for a full report
                    and will expect to telegraph the Department further in due course.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
